.TH BIO 2
.SH NAME
Reader, reader_init, read_line, read_all, read_file, Bio, bio_init, bio_write, bio_puts, bio_putc, bio_flush, eprint \- buffered input and output
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "vsys:libuser"
.PP
.nf
.B "Reader :: struct { fd: int, buf: [8192]u8, start: int, end: int, eof: bool }"
.B "reader_init :: proc(r: ^Reader, fd: int)"
.B "read_line :: proc(r: ^Reader) -> (line: string, ok: bool)"
.B "read_all :: proc(fd: int, allocator: runtime.Allocator) -> (data: []u8, ok: bool)"
.B "read_file :: proc(path: string, allocator: runtime.Allocator) -> (data: []u8, ok: bool)"
.B "eprint :: proc(parts: ..string)"
.PP
.B "Bio :: struct { fd: int, used: int, buf: [4096]u8 }"
.B "bio_init :: proc(b: ^Bio, fd: int)"
.B "bio_write :: proc(b: ^Bio, data: []u8) -> bool"
.B "bio_puts :: proc(b: ^Bio, s: string) -> bool"
.B "bio_putc :: proc(b: ^Bio, c: u8) -> bool"
.B "bio_flush :: proc(b: ^Bio) -> bool"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Reader
buffers a descriptor, 8192 bytes at a time, and hands out whole lines with
.IR read_line .
.I Reader_init
readies
.I r
to read from
.IR fd .
.I Read_line
answers the next line with its newline stripped, or
.B false
at the end of the file.
The last line of a file that does not end in a newline is a line too.
A line longer than the buffer is handed out whole, unterminated.
The string
.I read_line
answers is valid only until the next call: it aliases
.IR r 's
own buffer, and a caller that needs it kept makes a copy.
.PP
.I Read_all
reads a descriptor to its end into memory taken from
.IR allocator ,
for a tool that needs the whole input before it can start, such as
.IR sort (1)
or
.IR tail (1).
.I Read_file
opens
.I path
and is
.I read_all
of it.
Both answer
.B false
on any error, and the descriptor
.I read_file
opened is closed either way.
.PP
.I Eprint
writes its arguments to descriptor 2, one after another, as a single
message.
It does no formatting and no allocation: an error line is a few strings,
not a format string.
.PP
.B Bio
is a buffered writer, the output half of
.IR Reader .
.I Bio_init
readies
.I b
to write to
.IR fd .
.I Bio_write ,
.I bio_puts
and
.I bio_putc
copy bytes, a string or a byte into
.IR b 's
4096-byte buffer, flushing it to
.I fd
when it fills.
.I Bio_flush
writes whatever is buffered and empties it.
All four answer
.B false
when a flush failed, which means the descriptor is gone.
A program that writes with
.B Bio
and does not call
.I bio_flush
before it exits loses whatever is still buffered.
.SH EXAMPLES
.IR Uniq (1)
reads lines and writes them buffered:
.IP
.EX
r: libuser.Reader
libuser.reader_init(&r, fd)
out: libuser.Bio
libuser.bio_init(&out, 1)
for {
	line, ok := libuser.read_line(&r)
	if !ok {
		break
	}
	libuser.bio_write(&out, line)
	libuser.bio_putc(&out, '\en')
}
libuser.bio_flush(&out)
.EE
.PP
.IR Tail (1)
reads a whole file first:
.IP
.EX
data, ok := libuser.read_all(fd, context.allocator)
if !ok {
	libuser.exits("read error")
}
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/libuser/lines.odin
.br
.B sys/libuser/main.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR print (2),
.IR open (2),
.IR read (2),
.IR sort (1),
.IR tail (1),
.IR uniq (1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Every procedure here answers
.B false
or an empty result on failure.
None of them sets an error string; a caller that needs one calls
.IR errstr (2)
on the descriptor operation that failed.
